7. AI Layer (Free Tiers Only)

7.1 Router with cascade failover

```python
# core/ai/router.py
PROVIDERS = [
    ("groq",     "https://api.groq.com/openai/v1",                   "openai/gpt-oss-120b"),
    ("gemini",   "https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai/", "gemini-2.5-flash"),
    ("cerebras", "https://api.cerebras.ai/v1",                       "llama-3.3-70b"),
    ("mistral",  "https://api.mistral.ai/v1",                        "mistral-small-latest"),
]

async def ask(prompt: str, max_tokens: int = 256, json_mode: bool = False) -> str:
    for name, base, model in PROVIDERS:
        try:
            r = await client.post(
                f"{base}/chat/completions",
                headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {os.environ[f'{name.upper()}_API_KEY']}"},
                json={"model": model, "temperature": 0.1, "max_tokens": max_tokens,
                      "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": prompt}]},
                timeout=20,
            )
            if r.status_code == 200:
                return r.json()["choices"][0]["message"]["content"]
        except Exception:
            continue
    return ""
```

Caching: ai/cache.py keys on sha256(prompt) with TTL. Polymarket event analysis TTL = 1 hour. Reflection TTL = never (unique per trade). Cache hits avoid rate-limit exhaustion.

7.2 Layer 1 — Reflector (Week 2 onward)

After each closed losing trade, reflect_failed_trade() sends the trade record + current strategy params to the router and asks for one parameter change. The response is stored in reflections with accepted=0. A background job backtests the proposed variant; if it passes the gate, it becomes a new strategy_versions row.

```python
async def reflect_failed_trade(trade: dict, strategy: dict) -> dict:
    prompt = f"""Diagnose this failed trade and propose exactly ONE parameter change.
Trade: {json.dumps(trade)}
Current params: {json.dumps(strategy['params'])}
Ranges: {json.dumps(strategy['param_ranges'])}
Return JSON only: {{"diagnosis":"...","param":"...","old":...,"new":...}}"""
    raw = await ask(prompt, json_mode=True)
    try:
        return json.loads(raw)
    except Exception:
        return {"diagnosis": "parse_failed", "accepted": False}
```

7.3 Layer 2 — Event analyst (Polymarket)

Given a market title + optional recent news snippet, ask for a probability estimate in [0,1]. Compare to CLOB midpoint. If |estimate − midpoint| > 0.08 and time-to-resolution > 7 days, emit a poly_deviation signal candidate.

Guardrail: the analyst output cannot bypass the risk kernel or the minimum-liquidity guard. It only generates candidates.

7.4 Layer 3 — Fine-tuned reviewer (post-demo, Phase 5)

After 3 weeks of shadow trading, you have ~200–500 labeled trade records. Fine-tune an 8B open model (Llama 3.1 8B or Qwen 2.5) with QLoRA on a single 16–24GB GPU:

· Format: {"messages":[system, user: trade proposal + context, assistant: "REJECT. reason: ..."]}
· Labels: auto-derived from realized P&L + safety filter outcomes.
· Inference: a fast local model invoked before risk gate. Reject → BLOCKED with ai_reviewer.

Critical: the reviewer can only reject, never approve. Approval is always the risk engine's job. This preserves the hard caps even if the model is wrong.

